Group words by their sorted letters, sort each group, then sort groups by their first word. This preserves duplicates, handles empty strings and empty input, and does not mutate the input array.

```javascript
function solve(words) {
  const groups = new Map();

  for (const word of words) {
    const key = word.split('').sort().join('');
    if (!groups.has(key)) groups.set(key, []);
    groups.get(key).push(word);
  }

  const result = Array.from(groups.values());
  for (const group of result) group.sort();
  result.sort((a, b) =>
    a[0] < b[0] ? -1 : a[0] > b[0] ? 1 : 0
  );
  return result;
}
```

Call `solve(words)` directly; no imports, exports, or stdin/stdout are needed.

For n words and maximum word length L (taken as at least 1), time is O(nL log(L + 1) + nL log(n + 1)), accounting for string comparisons. Space is O(nL + n), including stored keys and output, under standard comparison-sort bounds.

